.TH SCHDRAW 1
.SH NAME
schdraw \- draw a schematic sheet
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B schdraw
[
.B \-v
] [
.B \-L
.I lib
] ...
.I sheet.sch
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Schdraw
reads a schematic source,
.IR sch (5),
places its parts as its statements say, routes the wires and writes
the page as
.IR vec (5)
on the standard output: the sheet template with its title block, each
net's wires and dots, and each part's symbol and text, the text as
text records.
The records of each part are bracketed by its name (the path of its
groups and its own, as ch1.gain.rf), with its box named, and those of
each net by the net's name, for
.IR schview (1).
The symbols and the sheet templates are those of
.IR schsym (5),
from the directories symbols and sheets next to the source, then
.IB lib /symbols
and
.IB lib /sheets
for each
.B \-L
option, or from
.B ../lib
next to the program.
With
.B \-v
it tells on stderr where each part was placed, by its line, its name
and designator (ch1.gain.rf (R401)) and the rule that placed it, once
the designators are known; its faults name parts so too.
The records of each part are bracketed by its name and designator.
.PP
The drawing follows the conventions of the figures of
.I The Art of Electronics:
one thin line weight, signal from left to right, supplies up and
ground down, filled dots at junctions and no four-way junctions,
designators in italic with the number as a subscript, values beside
the parts, all in Helvetica.
.PP
The pins of a net are wired in groups: a pin with a flag of its own
and the flag are a group, and the other pins of the net are one group
with the first of them.
A group is routed as a tree on the grid, 0.1 in at scale 1: first the
wires the source names, on their sides; then the two pins that face
each other on one line farthest apart, so the signal runs straight;
then every other pin takes the cheapest path to the tree, its nearest
first.
A path leaves and enters a pin along the way the pin points, runs
through no body and no other pin and along no wire of another group,
crosses other wires only at right angles, and joins its own tree only
where that makes a T.
Bends, running next to a body or a foreign wire, a
junction next to another, and straying off a said side cost more than
length; a crossing costs twice a step, so a wire crosses another
rather than going round it.
A group that finds no way is put first and all are routed again; once
all are routed, each is routed again among the others and the cheaper
path kept.
Each label then takes, among the places along each side of its part,
the one that covers least of the drawing, its own side and its middle
first.
.PP
Blocks that stand side by side across the sheet are spread to fill
it: a block is a run of parts, with their pins and labels, and wires
that nothing else of the drawing overlaps across the sheet, with the
flags (grounds, supplies, ports) that stand in it or next to it.
The free width of the room of the sheet template is shared equally by
the left margin, the right margin and each gap, so they come out alike,
a gap wider than its share closing up to it; if the share is under two
units of the grid, nothing moves.
Rows of blocks up the sheet are spread the same way, between the top of
the room and its foot above the title block.
No wire runs between two blocks, so they move whole; the drawing then
stands in the middle of the room, which keeps clear of the title
block.
.PP
Where each pin meets its wire is written as a pin record, by its
designator and number, and each flag's as a flag record, by its net, so
the drawing alone tells what it joins: the tests check it against the
netlist of
.IR schnet (1)
(a pin is on each wire that runs through or ends at it, two wires join
where the end of one lies on the other, flags of one name join; each
net must be one such group, no group may hold pins of two nets, and no
four wires may meet at a point).
.PP
The drawing keeps the room the clear records of the sheet template give
(schsym(5)), in points at scale 1; sch_a gives 3.6 between a wire and
the body of a part not on its net, and between two bodies; 1.5 between
a label and any wire, body or other label; 2.5 from a body to its own
label; wires of two nets keep a pitch of the grid apart, as the grid
gives them.
The placer keeps a branch hung from a lead (a decoupling capacitor and
its bead) out of a wired loop by that room and the thickness of its
parts, so the branch takes a longer lead and the loop keeps its shape;
the router keeps the room round every body; a label takes only a place
that keeps its room, if there is one.
Then schdraw measures every pair, and each that is too close is a
fault: FILE:LINE: and the two names.
.PP
For print,
.IR vecps (1)
makes PostScript of the page and
.IR ps2pdf (1)
a PDF.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
schdraw ch1.sch > ch1.vec && vecps ch1.vec > ch1.ps && ps2pdf ch1.ps
schview ch1.vec
schdraw -v ch1.sch 2>&1 >/dev/null | grep rf
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
sch(5), schsym(5), vec(5), schnet(1), netdiff(1), schview(1), vecps(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
FILE:LINE: and the fault, for a statement that cannot be followed or a
net that cannot be routed; each fault of the check on one line.
A fault of the source leaves out what it touches, and the rest is
drawn: a part that finds no place is not drawn, nor its wires.
Exit status 1 on any fault.
